RUBRIC:{"title":"AERO9500 Report Marking Rubric (Assignment Part 2)","total":null,"criteria":[{"id":"C1","name":"Introduction: mission concept and rationale - Include the following required information: • What is the spacecraft's primary mission? • Why is the mission needed? • How many spacecraft are used and why? • Who are the users (operator and customers)?","weight":10,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"Addresses all requirements to a high standard. Detailed descriptions and discussions are included. References to literature as appropriate."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Addresses some requirements. Descriptions may feature lack of specific details and/or incomplete referencing."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"Many or all requirements are not addressed. Poor descriptions or lack of appropriate referencing."}]},{"id":"C2","name":"Main body: mission design - Present a summary of the mission design using software and/or hand calculations as appropriate - Include relevant screenshots and software files (in the main body of the report and/or appendix) - Include the following information: • Where it will launch from, and using which launch vehicle? Why did you choose this option? • What type of orbit will you use? What are its characteristics and orbital elements? • Will you need to perform any orbital maneuvers? • Which ground control facilities will you use? • How often will your spacecraft be able to communicate with ground control? • How long will the mission last, and what are your plans for EOL?","weight":60,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"Demonstrates high level understanding of the design procedure. Design clearly addresses mission and course concepts. Major requirements are all addressed and are well supported with quality evidence (eg. software screenshots, software files, hand calculations, references from literature). Content is comprehensive, accurate, and persuasive."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Demonstrates adequate understanding of the design procedure. Design may be inconsistent with regards to purpose and clarity of thought or feature errors. Major requirements are addressed but may lack some detail or are not well supported with evidence. Content may not be comprehensive and/or persuasive."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"Demonstrates minimal understanding of the design procedure. Major requirements are not addressed and/or lack evidence. Content is incomplete or not persuasive."}]},{"id":"C3","name":"Conclusion","weight":10,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"Detailed and appropriate conclusions reached from the evidence provided in the report."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Some conclusions are reached but they may be generic or lack specific detail."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"No actual or useful conclusions reached."}]},{"id":"C4","name":"Report presentation","weight":20,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"The document follows a clear and logical engineering report structure. The report is easy to read: well-written, with good spelling and grammar, and appropriate language style. All aspects of formatting are consistent throughout the document. Executive summary (or abstract) provides a concise overview of the report. Graphical and tabular presentation of data is appropriate, clear and consistent. References in text match reference list (and vice versa) and are cited properly."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Document is not at a professional level but does make use of headings and subheadings to indicate document structure. The report may be difficult to read: writing is just ok, broad idea comes across; spelling and grammar have some flaws, not quite appropriate language style. Although figures and tables are labelled, the formatting is unclear and/or inconsistent to the extent that the reader can lose track of the context when reading. References in text match reference list (and vice versa) and are mostly cited correctly."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"The document is poorly structured, is not coherent or shows a lack of understanding of the purpose of its sections. Much effort is required to read and understand the report: writing is poor, many mistakes with spelling and grammar, and possibly inappropriate language style (e.g. too informal). Presentation is poor to the extent that it impedes reading of the document. Examples include inconsistent formatting, and unlabelled figures or tables. References are either not cited or cited inconsistently."}]}],"notes":"25% of course grade. Level score ranges are as printed (Accomplished 7–10, Adequate 4–6, Deficient 0–3). Weights are percentages of the assignment grade; no criterion max marks or overall total marks are printed."}
TASK:{"title":"AERO9500 Final Report (Part 2) – Space Mission Design","overview":"Students design a preliminary space mission benefiting Australia, covering the space segment: mission purpose, spacecraft count, users, launch site and vehicle, orbit and orbital elements, manoeuvres, ground control and communications, mission duration and end-of-life. Design may use STK, PIGI or approved software, supplemented by hand calculations, and is reported as a concise technical report with supporting screenshots and software files.","deliverables":["Single PDF technical report submitted via Turnitin on Moodle","Relevant screenshots included in the report or appendix","All relevant STK or PIGI (or other approved) software files included with the report or in the appendix"],"format":{"length":"Maximum 20 pages for the core part, excluding title page, table of contents, references, etc.","structure":"Executive summary; Introduction (mission overview and rationale); Main body covering all space segment sections (e.g. launch, orbit, ground stations, coverage, mission operation, end-of-life); Conclusion; Bibliography. Figures and tables fully and correctly labelled (figure number, title, reference if necessary).","other":"Clear, effective engineering-report writing style with correct grammar and spelling; clear and logical overall structure; all non-original text, diagrams and ideas clearly identified and properly cited and referenced; Wikipedia not accepted as a proper source; report checked for plagiarism."},"requirements":[{"id":"R1","text":"Design a space mission that benefits Australia in some way, keeping the mission concept simple (e.g. bushfire tracking/alert, weather satellite, internet/communication, Earth observation for city planning/real estate)."},{"id":"R2","text":"Identify and describe the main elements of the mission's space segment."},{"id":"R3","text":"State the spacecraft's primary mission."},{"id":"R4","text":"State whether one or multiple spacecraft will be used."},{"id":"R5","text":"Identify the users, including the operator and customers."},{"id":"R6","text":"Specify the launch site and the launch vehicle."},{"id":"R7","text":"Specify the orbit type and its characteristics and orbital elements."},{"id":"R8","text":"Identify any orbital manoeuvres required."},{"id":"R9","text":"Identify the ground control facilities to be used and state how often the spacecraft can communicate with ground control."},{"id":"R10","text":"State the mission duration and the plans for end-of-life (EOL)."},{"id":"R11","text":"Complete the design using software of choice (STK, PIGI, or other software such as flightclub only after contacting Naomi to discuss), supplementing with hand calculations as needed; if using PIGI, register for the competition to obtain a licence."},{"id":"R12","text":"Include relevant screenshots and all relevant STK or PIGI files with the report or in the appendix."},{"id":"R13","text":"Produce the report as a single PDF document with the required sections: executive summary, introduction, main body, conclusion, bibliography."},{"id":"R14","text":"Fully and correctly label all figures and tables (figure number, title, and reference if necessary)."},{"id":"R15","text":"Clearly identify and properly cite and reference all text, diagrams, ideas or other information not originating with the student; do not use Wikipedia as a proper source."}]}
